              <td>PWD / Orthopedic Double Allowance Multiplier</td>
              <td class="num" id="slipMultiplier">1.0x (Standard)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="font-weight: 800; background: var(--accent-green-bg);">
              <td style="color: var(--accent-green);">Net Monthly Transport Allowance Disbursed to Payslip</td>
              <td class="num" id="slipNetTotal" style="color: var(--accent-green);">₹5,508</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

    </section>

    <!-- IN-DEPTH 1,300+ WORDS FRESH SEO ARTICLE -->
    <article class="article-wrap">
      <h2>1. The Architecture of Transport Allowance in Central Civil Service</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Transport Allowance (TA)</strong> is a monthly compensatory disbursement provided to Central Government employees, defence personnel, and railway workers to offset commuting costs incurred between their place of residence and their official duty headquarters. Unlike travel allowances granted during official tours or temporary transfer postings, Transport Allowance is an automated, monthly recurring entitlement paid directly on the salary slip.
      </p>
      <p>
        The allowance is governed strictly by the Department of Expenditure (DoE), Ministry of Finance. It operates under a tiered framework determined by two factors: an employee's <strong>Pay Matrix Level</strong> and the <strong>geographic classification</strong> of their duty station (Higher TPTA Cities vs. Other Places).
      </p>

      <h2>2. Transport Allowance Slabs: Pay Matrix Brackets & City Classifications</h2>
      <p>
        The 7th Central Pay Commission simplified the older running Grade Pay groupings into three streamlined hierarchical tiers, each with distinct baseline rates:
      </p>

      <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Pay Matrix Level Tier</th>
              <th>Cadre / Administrative Representation</th>
              <th>Higher TPTA Cities (Base)</th>
              <th>Other Locations (Base)</th>
              <th>Total Disbursed (at 53% DA)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Level 9 and Above</strong></td>
              <td>Group-A Officers, SDM, Under Sec, Joint Sec (PB-2 GP 5400 to Level 18)</td>
              <td>₹7,200 + DA</td>
              <td>₹3,600 + DA</td>
              <td><strong>₹11,016 / ₹5,508</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Level 3 to Level 8</strong></td>
              <td>Constable, UDC, Auditor, Inspector, ASO, AAO (GP 2000 to GP 4800)</td>
              <td>₹3,600 + DA</td>
              <td>₹1,800 + DA</td>
              <td><strong>₹5,508 / ₹2,754</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Level 1 and Level 2</strong></td>
              <td>Multi-Tasking Staff (MTS), Support Staff, LDC (GP 1800 & GP 1900)</td>
              <td>₹1,350 + DA</td>
              <td>₹900 + DA</td>
              <td><strong>₹2,066 / ₹1,377</strong></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="formula-banner">
        Monthly Disbursed TA = Base TA Rate + [Base TA Rate × (Current Dearness Allowance % ÷ 100)]
      </div>

      <h2>3. The 19 Higher TPTA Urban Agglomerations (Qualifying Metros)</h2>
      <p>
        To qualify for the higher Transport Allowance slab, an employee's official duty station must fall within one of the <strong>19 Higher Transport Urban Agglomerations (UA)</strong> formally recognized by the Ministry of Finance:
      </p>

      <div class="tag-grid">
        {higherTptaCities.map((city) => (
          <span class="tag-pill">{city}</span>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>Suburban Expansion Rule:</strong> Under central service directives, if a central government establishment or military base is situated in an adjoining peripheral municipality or suburban cantonment that forms a continuous urban continuity with an authorized Higher TPTA city (e.g., Gurgaon, Noida, or Faridabad contiguous with Delhi), employees stationed at such establishments are entitled to the higher metropolitan TA rates.
      </p>

      <h2>4. PWD Concession: Double Transport Allowance for Employees with Disabilities</h2>
      <p>
        Central Civil Services rules extend specific statutory welfare protections to public servants with qualifying physical challenges:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Eligibility Categories:</strong> Government servants who are visually impaired (blind or low vision), orthopedically handicapped with locomotor disability of the lower limbs, or deaf/speech impaired.</li>
        <li><strong>The 2x Rate Benefit:</strong> Eligible personnel receive <strong>double the normal Transport Allowance</strong> rate applicable to their pay level and city tier:
          <br /><br />
          Total PWD TA = [2 × Base TA Rate] + [2 × Base TA Rate × (Current DA % ÷ 100)]
        </li>
        <li><strong>Minimum Statutory Floor:</strong> Regardless of pay scale, the base allowance for employees with disabilities cannot fall below a statutory minimum of <strong>₹2,250 + DA per month</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Income Tax Exemption:</strong> While normal TA is fully taxable, PWD employees can claim a statutory tax exemption of up to <strong>₹3,200 per month</strong> under Section 10(14) of the Income Tax Act (under the Old Tax Regime).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>5. Official Tour Travel Rules: Class Entitlements & Daily Allowance (DA)</h2>
      <p>
        When a government servant undertakes an authorized official temporary duty tour away from headquarters, travel reimbursements are governed by strict rank entitlements:
      </p>

      <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Pay Matrix Level</th>
              <th>Air Travel Eligibility</th>
              <th>Train Journey Entitlement</th>
              <th>Local City Transit Mode</th>
              <th>Hotel Ceiling (Per Day)</th>
              <th>Daily Food Reimbursement</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {travelEntitlements.map((t) => (
              <tr>
                <td><strong>{t.levels}</strong></td>
                <td>{t.air}</td>
                <td>{t.train}</td>
                <td>{t.road}</td>
                <td><strong style="color: var(--primary);">{t.hotel}</strong></td>
                <td>{t.food}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Reimbursement Rules for Daily Travel Allowances:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Absence Duration Calculation:</strong> If official absence from headquarters is less than 6 hours, <strong>30%</strong> of daily food allowance is payable. If between 6 and 12 hours, <strong>70%</strong> is payable. For absence exceeding 12 hours, <strong>100%</strong> of the daily allowance is disbursed.</li>
        <li><strong>Food Bill Requirements:</strong> Following 7th CPC procedural simplification, food allowance is disbursed as a lump-sum per diem based on the table rates above; submission of hotel dining vouchers or restaurant bills is no longer mandatory.</li>
        <li><strong>Hotel Accommodation Receipts:</strong> Production of valid commercial hotel tax invoices bearing the hotel's GST registration number is mandatory to claim lodging reimbursement up to the ceiling limits.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>6. Composite Transfer Grant (CTG): Relocation on Transfer / Retirement</h2>
      <p>
        When a government servant is transferred to a new station for administrative reasons, or relocates following superannuation retirement, they are entitled to the <strong>Composite Transfer Grant (CTG)</strong> alongside personal and family travel passage:
      </p>

      <div class="formula-banner">
        Standard CTG = 80% of Last Drawn Monthly Basic Pay (Transfers involving change of station &gt; 20 km)
      </div>

      <h3>Special Transfer Provisions:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Island Territories Exception:</strong> For transfers to or from the Andaman & Nicobar Islands or Lakshadweep, the CTG is payable at <strong>100% of last drawn basic pay</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Short-Distance Transfers (&le; 20 km):</strong> If a transfer does not involve a change of residence, no CTG is payable. If an employee is compelled to change their residential dwelling within the same urban area due to transfer beyond 20 km, a reduced CTG of <strong>one-third (33.33%)</strong> of basic pay is admissible.</li>
        <li><strong>Personal Effects Transport:</strong> In addition to the CTG, employees are reimbursed for personal household luggage transport by train (full 4-wheeler wagon or container load) or by road through accredited logistics carriers up to prescribed weight limits (e.g., 6,000 kg for Levels 6 and above).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>7. Leave Travel Concession (LTC) Passage Guidelines</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>Leave Travel Concession (LTC)</strong> allows central employees and their families to visit their declared "Home Town" twice in a four-year block, or visit "Anywhere in India" once in lieu of one Home Town visit:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Air Travel on LTC:</strong> Only employees serving in <strong>Level 9 and above</strong> are entitled to travel by air on official LTC. Officers must book tickets exclusively through three authorized travel agents: Balmer Lawrie & Co., Ashok Travels & Tours, or IRCTC.</li>
        <li><strong>Special Relaxations:</strong> Non-entitled personnel (Levels 1 to 8) are periodically allowed to travel by air to specific designated regions (Jammu & Kashmir, Ladakh, Andaman & Nicobar, and the North Eastern Region) under special government relaxation orders.</li>
        <li><strong>Encashment of Earned Leave:</strong> Employees can encash up to 10 days of earned leave at the time of availing LTC, subject to a lifetime career maximum of 60 days across their entire service.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>8. The 8th Pay Commission Reset: Projected Transport Allowance Slabs</h2>
      <p>
        When the 8th Central Pay Commission takes effect:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>DA Reset to 0%:</strong> The current 53%+ DA will be absorbed into basic pay, causing the "DA on TA" component to temporarily reset to zero.</li>
        <li><strong>Recalibration of Base TA Rates:</strong> To compensate for higher fuel, electricity, and urban transit costs, the baseline TA rates are projected to increase to approximately <strong>₹16,200</strong> (Level 9+), <strong>₹8,100</strong> (Levels 3–8), and <strong>₹3,000</strong> (Levels 1–2) in Higher TPTA cities.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>9. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">
        {faqs.map((faq) => (
          <div class="faq-unit">
            <div class="faq-quest">
              <span>{faq.q}</span>
              <span class="faq-arrow">▼</span>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-ans">{faq.a}</div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </article>

